---
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import PageTitle from '../../../components/PageTitle.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import CiteBlock from '../../../components/CiteBlock.astro';
import Factsheet from '../../../components/Factsheet.astro';
import core from '../../../data/core.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';
import { buildDate } from '../../../data/build';

const c = core.counts;
const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'buddhist-bridges')!;
const rows = [
  { term: 'Contents', html: `${c.people_total} people examined, of which ${c.people_carried} are carried after Buddhist-relevance classification · ${c.travels} travels · ${c.texts} texts · ${c.institutions} institutions · ${c.events} events · ${c.sources} graded sources.` },
  { term: 'Release', html: 'v3.0, frozen 17 August 2026. 13 CSVs under a SHA-256 manifest (<span class="mono">769b00c67fdd1a6e</span>), rebuilt deterministically from plain-text source modules.' },
  { term: 'Downloads', html: '<a href="/data/people.json" download>people.json</a> · <a href="/data/story.json" download>story.json</a> · <a href="/data/timeline.json" download>timeline.json</a> · <a href="/data/sources.json" download>sources.json</a>' },
  { term: 'Licensing', html: 'Dataset CC BY 4.0 · code MIT.' },
  { term: 'Build', html: `Site built ${buildDate} from the v3.0 export.` },
];
---
<Project title="Buddhist Bridges — Method and data"
  description="Inclusion criteria, confidence grading, reproducibility, limits, licence and citation for the Buddhist Bridges dataset v3.0.">
  <PageTitle title="Method and data"
    lede="What counts as a record, how confidence is graded, how the release is verified, and how to cite it." />
  <section class="band band--flush">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="small muted breadcrumb"><a href={p.href}>← {p.shortTitle}</a></p>

      <section class="section first">
        <h2>The rule of collection</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="rule-line">
            No relationship is recorded as influential without documentary evidence; association with both
            countries is not influence.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every record carries a confidence grade, a completeness status and a date-precision field. Records
            without a safe date are never placed on the timeline axis; they are listed in the register as
            undated. Rejected candidates stay in the dataset as documented negatives rather than being deleted,
            so the boundary of a claim can be inspected. Legendary material is labelled and excluded from
            analysis by default.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Confidence grading</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <dl class="grades">
            <div><dt><span class="badge high">HIGH</span></dt><dd>Corroborated by two or more independent sources of academic or archival standing.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="badge medium">MEDIUM</span></dt><dd>Documented, but resting on fewer or weaker sources.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="badge low">single source</span></dt><dd>The record is in the dataset and is not corroborated. Shown hatched wherever it is drawn.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="small muted">
            Confidence grades the source basis, not historical importance. A well-known figure with thin
            documentation grades lower than an obscure one with two archival attestations, and that is the
            intended behaviour.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The frozen release is rebuilt deterministically from plain-text source modules. This site reads
            only a JSON export that verifies the manifest before every build, so the site cannot silently
            change the data: a checksum mismatch aborts the export rather than publishing a changed figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Uncertainty is drawn rather than footnoted, using the grammar shared across this site: dashed
            floruit bars, hatched single-source lives, an annotated void where the evidence bar admits nothing.
            The shared grammar is set out on the <a href="/method/">method page</a>. The gap it addresses is
            documented: a 2024 survey of 186 web-based Digital Humanities projects found that 122 use maps, 12
            critically adapt them, and none visualises uncertainty. See Tommaso Battisti, <i>Classification of
            web-based Digital Humanities projects leveraging information visualisation techniques</i> (Zenodo,
            2024), <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14192758">doi:10.5281/zenodo.14192758</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>At a glance</h2>
        <div class="measure"><Factsheet rows={rows} /></div>
        <Apparatus label="Repository and deposit">
          <p>
            The dataset is prepared for deposit: the release is frozen, checksummed and documented. The
            repository and a Zenodo DOI follow, and this page will carry the DOI when it is minted. Until then
            the version string above, with its manifest checksum, identifies the exact state of the evidence.
          </p>
        </Apparatus>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Limits</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <ul>
            <li>All analysis is descriptive (n = {c.people_carried}).</li>
            <li>The modern era is documented mainly through the press, a different evidentiary regime from the premodern chapters.</li>
            <li>Korean-language sources dominate; an Indian-source corroboration round has not been carried out.</li>
            <li>No woman appears in the record before 1990. The register records that absence; it cannot by itself explain it.</li>
            <li>Only {c.journeys_routed} of {c.travels} travels have routes documented well enough to map, which is why no map is published.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2 id="cite">Cite</h2>
        <CiteBlock
          citation="Bilal, Mohd. Five Bridges, One Silence: Documented Mediators of India–Korea Buddhist Exchange, 300–2026. Dataset v3.0, frozen 17 August 2026."
          licence="Dataset CC BY 4.0 · code MIT"
          url="https://mohdbilaldh.github.io/projects/buddhist-bridges/"
          buildDate={buildDate} />
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Project>
<style>
  .breadcrumb { margin: 0 0 24px; }
  .first { margin-top: 0; }
  .rule-line { font-style: italic; border-left: 3px solid var(--yellow); padding-left: 20px; }
  .grades > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr; gap: 16px; padding: 10px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
  .grades dt { margin: 0; }
  .grades dd { margin: 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .grades > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; } }
</style>
